{"id": "pa-00087", "model": "minicpm5", "cell": "36", "catalog_size": 36, "sampling": "greedy", "machine": "laptop", "server": {"model_path": "~/Comet/models/MiniCPM5-2B-Q4_K_M.gguf", "build": "b12555-c406f4367"}, "calls": [], "content": "Here are some practical tips for sharp, glare-free photos of flat paper documents:\n\n1. **Natural Light**: Shoot during bright, even daylight. Avoid backlighting (lighting from the paper's edge) to prevent glare.\n\n2. **Flat Background**: Keep the paper close to a plain surface (e.g., a white wall or a clean desk). A blurred background can help separate the paper from the surface.\n\n3. **Fill the Frame**: Use the entire frame to capture the document. Avoid leaving large, empty areas that might look like shadows.\n\n4. **Even Exposure**: Use a single light source (like a window) to avoid uneven lighting. If using flash, use a soft, diffuse flash to avoid harsh reflections.\n\n5. **Clean Paper**: Ensure the paper is clean and free of dust or smudges. A slightly damp paper towel can help, but be careful not to introduce water.\n\n6. **Focus Carefully**: Use manual focus if possible. Tap the focus ring gently to avoid disturbing the paper.\n\n7. **Use a Tripod**: A stable surface helps maintain focus and reduces camera shake.\n\n8. **Post-Processing**: If needed, use a slight crop to avoid including unwanted shadows.\n\nWould you like me to help with anything else related to this, such as setting up a note with these tips or checking your camera settings?", "format_error": false, "finish_reason": "stop", "prompt_tokens": 3538, "completion_tokens": 281, "cached_tokens": 0, "prompt_ms": 42779.568, "decode_ms": 20812.827, "decode_tok_per_s": 13.453242079992304, "wall_ms": 63712.627534987405}
